      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are DeerRun treadmills any good?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For walking, generally yes — a slim deck running at 2 to 3 mph is not a demanding engineering problem, and the price is genuinely low. For jogging it depends entirely on deck length, and for sustained running the category is not built for it. The hardware is usually appropriate to the price; what differs from a traditional brand is everything after delivery.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is an Amazon-native treadmill brand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A brand that exists as a marketplace storefront and a trademark rather than as a manufacturer with dealers, a parts catalogue and technicians. Production is contracted to factories building similar machines for many badges. Removing dealer margin and a service network takes real money out of the price — and takes the parts channel and repair support out with it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do these treadmills have thousands of good reviews?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because reviews cluster in the first few weeks, when what is being measured is delivery, assembly and first impressions — all things these machines do well. Durability generates far fewer reviews, since a failure in month fourteen arrives after the reviewer has moved on. Read recent one and two-star reviews for the information that matters, and check they describe the current product.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you get parts for a DeerRun treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Mechanical parts, usually yes — belts are sized by width and length and are generic, and rollers, drive belts and switches are commodity items. Electronic parts are the problem: a controller board for a discontinued marketplace model is often unavailable at any price. That is the structural difference from a brand with a real parts catalogue, and it is why lubrication matters so much here.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a cheap walking pad worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be a genuinely rational purchase. A working machine you own beats a better one you did not buy, and finding out whether you will actually use a treadmill for a couple of hundred dollars rather than fifteen hundred is sensible. Treat it as semi-disposable, use the returns window properly, and lubricate it from month one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I judge a marketplace treadmill listing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Find the continuous-duty motor rating rather than the peak figure, the running surface dimensions rather than the overall size, the minimum speed if you plan to walk slowly, and the assembled weight, which indicates frame rigidity better than the capacity rating does. Then read recent negative reviews, note the returns deadline, and ask the seller whether replacement belts and boards exist.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-18",
