        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The hardware is usually fine for walking and the low price is real. What you are giving up
            is <strong class="text-white">everything downstream of delivery</strong> — no parts
            catalogue, no technician, and a support trail that can go cold when the model is
            retired.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy one to walk on, or to find out cheaply whether you will use a treadmill at all. Use the
            returns window properly,
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">lubricate from month one</a>
            because the board is the part you cannot replace, and check what
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">the same money buys secondhand</a>
            before ordering.
          </p>
